tamework.ui.guide.topic.gettingStarted.title=Getting started
tamework.ui.guide.topic.gettingStarted.body=Start with one animal\nLook at the animal and read its prompt. It may ask for food, an item, or another action to tame it. Different animals can need different things.\n\nTame, own, and select\nTaming lets an animal become a companion. Ownership means it belongs to you. Ordinary command flutes show your owned companions automatically. Select an animal on each flute that should command it; selection is separate from ownership.\n\nGive your first command\nUse a command flute that works with your animal. Select the animal, open the flute's menu, and choose Follow. Make sure it is selected, then use the command.\n\nGive it a name\nUse a naming item on an animal you own. Type a name into the box.\n\nIf something does not work\nRead the prompt again. Check your held item, ownership, and any timer. Your animal mod decides which items and actions you can use.
tamework.ui.guide.topic.gettingStarted.visual=Meet your companion
tamework.ui.guide.topic.gettingStarted.note=Start with an animal you own. Open an ordinary command flute, select it, and try Follow.
tamework.ui.guide.topic.readingCard.title=Reading a card
tamework.ui.guide.topic.readingCard.body=This card tells you about your animal\nIts name and picture help you find the right animal. Health shows how much damage it can take. Level, traits, and talent points show its progress.\n\nCheck its needs\nHappiness, hunger, and thirst tell you how your animal is doing. Higher hunger and thirst bars mean it has more food and water left. Hover over a bar or icon for more information.\n\nUse the buttons\nEach button acts on that animal. A button may disappear or be unavailable when the animal cannot use it.\n\nFind the animals you want\nThe panel shows all owned companions. Tabs separate In World, Stored, Lost / Dead, and All; selected companions appear first. Nearby only narrows the view, and one search field matches name, species, or group. These browsing controls do not change selection.\n\nFar-away animals\nTheir cards may show the last saved information. See Finding & recovery if you cannot find one.\n\nAge and readiness meters\nThe menu and target HUD can show age, breeding, and harvest meters. Age progress fills within the current life stage, then resets for the next stage. Timers use hours and minutes, then seconds below one minute. A pause symbol marks paused timers.\n\nStars and capacity\nSome animal mods show quality stars at the top-right of the portrait and an animal capacity summary in the command-menu header. The mod decides what the rating means.\n\nRead the status tabs\nIn World includes loaded and unloaded animals. Stored includes captured animals and coop residents. Lost / Dead shows animals needing recovery or revival. All combines these views. Each tab counts animals matching your current search and Nearby only filter; these are browsing counts, not the number selected for commands.
tamework.ui.guide.topic.readingCard.visual=Your animal's card
tamework.ui.guide.topic.readingCard.note=Hover over the real card controls below. The names and numbers are made up for this guide.
tamework.ui.guide.topic.commandsGroups.title=Commands & groups
tamework.ui.guide.topic.commandsGroups.body=Choose a command\nOpen your tool's menu and pick a command for an input slot. Picking a command does not use it yet. Use that input while holding the tool to give the command.\n\nChoose who listens\nOrdinary flutes show your owned companions automatically. Select recipients with the checkboxes on their cards, or click an owned animal with the flute. Each physical flute remembers its own selection. Clear selection empties that flute's selected set without giving up ownership.\n\nBrowse without changing recipients\nStatus tabs, Nearby only, search, and sorting change what you see, not who receives commands. Selected animals can be hidden by a filter. A specialized flute may not support every selected animal; check the card for restrictions.\n\nCreate and edit shared groups\nGroups belong to you and are shared across compatible flutes. Use Add group to choose a name and color. An animal can belong to several groups. Use its group picker to toggle memberships inline, then close it. Editing a group is separate from selecting command recipients.\n\nSelect one group or combine groups\nLeft-click a group shortcut to replace this flute's selection with its members. Right-click to add its members while keeping existing selections. A group is highlighted when all its eligible members are selected; several groups can be highlighted together.\n\nWhat do commands do?\nFollow brings animals with you. Hold tells them to stay put. Idle uses their normal idle behavior. Defend and Aggressive change how they fight. Attack Target sends them after a target. Move To Ping sends them to the spot you choose. Available commands depend on the tool.\n\nIf an animal ignores you\nCheck its selection, tool compatibility, and card status. Animals in an item or coop must be released first.
tamework.ui.guide.topic.commandsGroups.visual=Choose who listens
tamework.ui.guide.topic.commandsGroups.note=Selection is saved on each flute. Groups are shared; left-click replaces selection and right-click adds to it.
tamework.ui.guide.topic.careHappiness.title=Care & happiness
tamework.ui.guide.topic.careHappiness.body=Keep food and water topped up\nThe hunger bar shows how much food your animal has left. The thirst bar shows how much water it has left. Feed it, give it water, or refill a trough it can use.\n\nWatch happiness\nFood, water, petting, and other things can affect happiness. Hover over happiness to see more details. It may take time to rise after you improve the animal's care.\n\nWhat is the red mark?\nThe small red mark shows the happiness needed for breeding. Reach the mark to pass that check. Your animal still needs a partner and must meet the other breeding rules.\n\nDo not leave needs empty\nLow food or water can make animals unhappy or stop some actions. Some servers also let animals take damage if their needs stay very low.\n\nReady to harvest?\nSome animals give you things such as milk. Their timer shows when you can harvest again. Read the animal's prompt when it is ready.\n\nUnderstand happiness details\nThe tooltip explains current, base, and target happiness. Colored effects are active; gray effects are configured but inactive.\n\nWhen the area is unloaded\nFood and water needs and trough consumption pause. Harvest readiness can advance to one ready harvest, but does not stockpile repeated harvests. Return to a loaded animal to perform the action. Your server's Animal Progression settings decide which systems continue while the owner is offline.\n\nServer presets\nAdministrators can use /tw settings to choose an experience preset. Selecting a preset fills the form immediately; review the values and use Apply to save them.\n\nHardcore care\nHardcore enables full adult aging and old-age death for supported animals, disables revives and recall teleportation, and sets starvation and dehydration damage to 10% and 15% of maximum health per minute. The other standard presets restore revives and recall teleportation and turn off old-age death. Custom server settings may differ.
tamework.ui.guide.topic.careHappiness.visual=Check food, water, and happiness
tamework.ui.guide.topic.careHappiness.note=A low food or water bar means it is time to help. Hover over happiness to see what is affecting it.
tamework.ui.guide.topic.breedingGrowth.title=Breeding & growth
tamework.ui.guide.topic.breedingGrowth.body=Get both parents ready\nChoose two animals that can breed together. They usually need to be adults. Turn breeding on, keep them near each other, and make sure they are happy enough.\n\nRead the breeding timer\nA timer means you need to wait before that animal can breed again. Ready means its timer has finished. It still needs a suitable partner.\n\nWhy are there no babies?\nCheck both parents. Breeding may be off, happiness may be too low, or one parent may be too young. Too many nearby animals or your server's land rules can also stop breeding.\n\nBabies and growth\nBabies may look smaller or have fewer actions until they grow up. How long they take depends on the animal. They can inherit traits from their parents.\n\nMore than one baby\nFertility can affect how many babies are born. Some pairings can have none, while others can have several. Nearby animal limits can reduce the number.\n\nGrowing into adulthood\nYoung animals hide breeding controls and timers. Reaching adulthood adds no extra offspring breeding cooldown, but normal partner, happiness, and population checks still apply.\n\nAdult life stages\nSome species keep aging as adults. By default, adult aging stops in the prime stage. An animal mod can enable later stages, changes to yields, and death from old age. Check the age meter and tooltip for that animal.\n\nAway from the farm\nLow food or water can slow young animals' growth and make older animals age faster. While the area is unloaded, aging follows server timing rules without care penalties. Breeding readiness can finish once, but births need the area loaded; repeated litters do not build up in the background.
tamework.ui.guide.topic.breedingGrowth.visual=Is this parent ready?
tamework.ui.guide.topic.breedingGrowth.note=Check both parents. Being happy enough is only one part of getting ready to breed.
tamework.ui.guide.topic.traitsTalents.title=Traits & talents
tamework.ui.guide.topic.traitsTalents.body=Traits are your animal's qualities\nTraits can change things such as health, strength, speed, size, or fertility. An animal may be born with them or inherit them from its parents. Hover over each trait icon to see what it does.\n\nLevels show progress\nAnimals that can level up earn XP from activities chosen by their animal mod. Enough XP raises their level.\n\nSpend talent points\nIf the card shows spare talent points, open Talents. Pick a talent to see its effect and what it needs. Some talents must be unlocked before you can buy the next one. Only use Reset if you want to undo your choices; read any rules shown first.\n\nWhen the animal is far away\nAn unloaded animal normally needs to load before you can spend its points. Dead or LOST animals can sometimes use their saved talent page before recovery. This needs a complete saved copy of the animal.\n\nNot every animal has every feature\nYour animal mod decides which traits, levels, and talents are available.\n\nSize and harvest yields\nThe size trait tooltip shows its meat and hide yield bonus. Any portrait quality stars follow your animal mod's rating rules; they are separate from level and talent points.
tamework.ui.guide.topic.traitsTalents.visual=Traits, levels, and talents
tamework.ui.guide.topic.traitsTalents.note=Hover over a trait to read its effect. Use the Talents button on your own animal's card to spend points.
tamework.ui.guide.topic.captureCoops.title=Capture & coops
tamework.ui.guide.topic.captureCoops.body=Carry an animal in an item\nUse a capture item that works with your animal. After capture, the filled item holds that animal. Use the filled item's release action to let it out.\n\nCapture is not a heal\nAn animal keeps its current health when captured. You cannot capture a dead animal.\n\nWhat does it keep?\nThe animal can keep its name, appearance, and progress through capture and release. Some animal mods change who owns it after release, so check their rules.\n\nUse a coop\nA coop can hold animals it accepts. Follow its prompt to put animals in or let them out. Some coops also accept a filled capture item if there is room.\n\nLook at the card\nCaptured means the animal is in an item. In Coop means it is inside a coop. Use that item or coop to release it. Recall will not take it out.\n\nLost the item?\nSee Finding & recovery to learn about last-seen locations.\n\nTime in storage\nCapture items pause aging and readiness timers. Captured cards retain the remaining time and show pause symbols.\n\nCoop production\nCoop timers can continue under your server's progression rules. Managed coops can catch up production using saved eligible animal time, within their container capacity and processing limits. Time paused in a capture item does not produce extra goods.\n\nCaptured cards and trading\nAn animal already tracked by your flute can remain visible after capture clears its ownership. Compatible filled capture items in your inventory can also appear as read-only stored cards. A card does not mean you still own the animal or can command it. When another player successfully releases and acquires it, the former owner's flute records are removed. Failed releases keep the existing records.
tamework.ui.guide.topic.captureCoops.visual=The same animal, safely stored
tamework.ui.guide.topic.captureCoops.note=Captured animals must leave their item. Animals in a coop must leave the coop. Recall does not replace either step.
tamework.ui.guide.topic.findingRecovery.title=Finding & recovery
tamework.ui.guide.topic.findingRecovery.body=Read the status first\nUnloaded means your animal is not loaded near you. It does not mean it is dead.\nCaptured means it is in an item. In Coop means it is housed in a coop.\nDead means the game recorded its death.\nLOST means the game has a saved animal it may be able to recover after it was removed.\n\nFind it\nLocate can show a place, an item, a chest, or the player carrying it. Last seen means the information may be old. Unknown means there is no useful location yet.\n\nBring it back\nRecall may take time if an animal is far away. If the attempt ends, it does not make the animal LOST.\nFor Dead or LOST animals, check the Revive or Recover action. Wait for any timer and read any item cost.\n\nBe careful with removal\nFor ordinary owned companions, clear selection to stop commanding them; they remain in your owned list. Legacy tools may offer Unlink to remove a tool link.\nRelease permanently removes the companion and frees its ownership slot. Loaded animals are removed immediately. Captured animals and coop occupants must leave storage first. This is different from letting an animal out of a capture item or coop.\nCull kills the animal.\nRead the confirmation before choosing a permanent action.\n\nCopy a location\nOn cards with coordinates, use the copy button immediately to their left. It opens a selectable field with a hint below it. Saved locations may be out of date.\n\nRecall is not revival\nRecall does not bring a dead animal back to life. Use the offered Revive action and meet its requirements.\n\nCheck the server rules\nRecovery and revival are different actions. Hardcore disables revives and recall teleportation; other custom settings can also restrict these features. Read the action offered on the card instead of assuming every dead or distant animal can return.
tamework.ui.guide.topic.findingRecovery.visual=Find the right next step
tamework.ui.guide.topic.findingRecovery.note=An animal that is far away is not automatically dead or lost. Read its status before choosing an action.
tamework.ui.guide.topic.bondedCompanions.title=Bonded companions
tamework.ui.guide.topic.bondedCompanions.body=A list of companions you can summon\nBonded companions use their own list. Use All, Active, Stored, and Dead to find the one you want.\n\nSummon and dismiss\nSummon brings a stored companion into the world. Dismiss puts an active companion away again.\nThere may be a limit on how many you can summon at once. Some summons also have a time limit or a wait before you can summon again.\n\nRead the card\nAn active companion shows its current health and progress. A stored companion shows its saved information. A dead companion has an empty health bar.\nThese companions do not show the usual food, water, and happiness bars.\n\nBring back a dead companion\nUse Revive when it is available. You may need to wait or pay the items shown in the confirmation.\n\nRemove one forever\nThe card's removal button asks you to confirm before deleting the companion. Choose Cancel if you want to keep it.
tamework.ui.guide.topic.bondedCompanions.visual=Summon, store, or revive
tamework.ui.guide.topic.bondedCompanions.note=Stored companions use Summon. Active companions use Dismiss. Dead companions may be able to use Revive.
tamework.ui.guide.topic.travelFlight.title=Travel & flight
tamework.ui.guide.topic.travelFlight.body=Set a home\nSet Home remembers a home spot for an animal you command. Return Home sends it there. Recall brings an animal back to you when the tool and animal allow it.\n\nTravelling far away\nAn animal in another area or world may take time to return, or may not be allowed to travel there. An animal inside an item or coop must be released first.\n\nRide or carry an animal\nRead its prompt to mount it or use shoulder riding. Some animals need you to crouch first. Flight buttons on an animal's card change that animal's mode.\n\nFlying as your mount\nAvatar flight lets you control the flying animal's form. Select Flightmaster's Talisman before starting. The flight HUD shows the controls you can use.\n\nTake off and stay in the air\nWith charged launch, hold crouch on the ground, then release after charging. Flaps and boosts use Vigour. Fast flight can refill it. Glide or dive to build speed, and climb to trade speed for height.\nChanging worlds ends avatar flight.\n\nRecall restrictions\nRecall teleportation can be disabled by the server and is disabled in the Hardcore preset. Check the server rules before relying on it to bring distant animals back.
tamework.ui.guide.topic.travelFlight.visual=Home and travel controls
tamework.ui.guide.topic.travelFlight.note=Set Home remembers a place. Return Home sends your animal there. Recall brings it back to you.
tamework.ui.guide.topic.worldUtilities.title=World utilities
tamework.ui.guide.topic.worldUtilities.body=Place a scarecrow\nChoose a solid surface and place the scarecrow. To pick it up, look at it and hold the shown key for one second. Make room in your inventory first.\n\nWhat does it stop?\nA scarecrow stops automatic spawn markers within 32 blocks. Regular world spawning is also blocked in the nearby spawn areas that touch that space, so the edge may reach a little farther.\n\nWhat does it leave alone?\nAnimals already there stay there. Breeding, capture items, commands, and Recall can still bring animals into the area.\nTwo overlapping scarecrows keep working separately. Removing one does not turn off the other.\n\nTroughs\nKeep food and water supplied for animals that can use the trough. See Care & happiness for help.\n\nCoops\nUse a coop's prompts to put animals in or let them out. See Capture & coops for help.
tamework.ui.guide.topic.worldUtilities.visual=Looking after your animals
tamework.ui.guide.topic.worldUtilities.note=A scarecrow does not remove animals already nearby. Use troughs for care and coops for storage.

